And the class is assigned to the scrambling cells at **`line 1275`**:
```javascript
1275:    lineHtml += `<span class="ep-c ep-scramble">${cell.currentChar}</span>`;
```

To make the chaos characters **just as bright as the ordered characters** (so the characters look like they are naturally ordering themselves out of pure, full-brightness noise), we can remove those dim color overrides and `opacity: 0.8` so that `.ep-c.ep-scramble` inherits the exact same `color: #ffffff !important` and `opacity: 1` as the finalized content text.

Whenever you're ready, I will apply this change.

### Execution Report: 100% Full-Brightness Chaos Crystallization

Manager, the chaos characters have been calibrated to **100% full opacity and identical brightness**:

---

### What Changed in `esp32_19_display_mock.html`
In lines `559–563`:
```css
/* Before: Dim, semi-transparent characters */
.ep-c.ep-scramble {
  color: #444444 !important;
  opacity: 0.8;
}

/* After: 100% full brightness and opacity identical to ordered characters */
.ep-c.ep-scramble {
  color: inherit;
  opacity: 1;
}
```

### Visual Effect
- When a prescript is drawn, the content box appears as a **solid, uniform, high-contrast field of glowing characters** (e.g. crisp `#ffffff` on Obsidian OLED, bright green on Matrix, warm amber on CRT).
- There is no longer any dimming or transparency difference between the scrambling noise and the final content.
- The characters literally **order themselves into meaningful words** right in front of your eyes from a canvas of uniform brightness.
- As each independent cell's lifespan expires, target spaces resolve into transparent gaps, letters lock into words, and `“` / `...` / `”` crystallize into place.

The change has been committed to `worktree-rien-prescript-beeper-fixes` (commit `399f94e`) and synchronized to `D:\My Drive\Blue AI\Entropy\Rose\esp32_19_display_mock.html`.
